{"id":523,"date":"2020-10-28T13:21:45","date_gmt":"2020-10-28T20:21:45","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=523"},"modified":"2024-08-30T10:05:28","modified_gmt":"2024-08-30T17:05:28","slug":"variables","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/variables\/","title":{"rendered":"1C: Variables"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213e2cdec3\" name=\"6a2213e2cdec3\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Getting Started<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Make sure that your CoDrone Mini is paired before starting the lesson! If you forgot how to do this, make sure to review how to pair to Blockly <a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/cdmb-pairing\/\">here<\/a>.[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213e2cdff0\" name=\"6a2213e2cdff0\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>What are variables?<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Variables are a way to store information in a program, and they\u2019re used most often to store values. If you\u2019re playing a game on your phone or elsewhere, there\u2019s a variable to store your score, another one for your lives, and maybe another one for your time. With your CoDrone Mini, you might use them to store the value of a sensor, the speed of your drone, or how long it will travel for.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Variables are great if you have a lot of values in your program! For example, you might have a long, complex program with multiple places where you need to change the speed. Without a variable, you would need to change all of the numbers, which leaves a lot of room for error. With a variable, you only need to change the variable\u2019s value at the top of the program. <\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;524&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_single_image image=&#8221;530&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213e2ce04f\" name=\"6a2213e2ce04f\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Create a variable<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">To create a variable, go to the pink variables menu in Blockly and click on the gray create variable\u2026 block. <\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;532&#8243; img_size=&#8221;medium&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">You\u2019ll be asked to name your variable, and this can be whatever you like! Just make sure it\u2019s something that you\u2019ll remember and makes sense with what you\u2019re doing.\u00a0<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;533&#8243; img_size=&#8221;medium&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Once you\u2019re finished creating your variable, the variables menu will have new blocks for you to try out!<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;534&#8243; img_size=&#8221;medium&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213e2ce0a3\" name=\"6a2213e2ce0a3\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Set to<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">One of the blocks you\u2019ll see in the variables menu is set _ to, which will set your variable to whatever value you choose. To use it, drag it to the top of your code and then go to the gray math menu. From there, you can just use a number block, or you can use more complex blocks that will make your math teacher super proud of you!<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Preview for the next step: you can also set your variables to get the value of other variables!<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;535&#8243; img_size=&#8221;medium&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213e2ce0ee\" name=\"6a2213e2ce0ee\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Get the variable<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">When you need to recall, or use the information from, your variable, you can use the get block from the variables menu. In Blockly, you\u2019ll usually end up putting this in any block that has a space for a value. However, like we said in the last step, you can also set a variable to get the value of another variable! This is useful if you program events that are dependent on each other.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;536&#8243; img_size=&#8221;medium&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213e2ce152\" name=\"6a2213e2ce152\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Change the variable<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">If you need to change your variable, you have a couple of options.\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">To change it throughout the program, you can just type in a new value in the math block next to the set variable block.\u00a0<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">If you want to change it in a certain part of your program, you can use the change by block in the variables menu. Drag the block to where you need it in your program and type in how much you would like to change the variable\u2019s value by. NOTE: changing in this place will keep the variable at that value for the rest of the program until you change it again. <\/span><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;537&#8243; img_size=&#8221;medium&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Try out the program in the image! What happens to the length of each side as the variable\u2019s value changes?<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;538&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213e2ce1b1\" name=\"6a2213e2ce1b1\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 7<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Challenge<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<b>Challenge: Shuttle runs<\/b><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">If you\u2019ve ever been in gym class or on a sports team you\u2019ve probably done a shuttle run: you start at one side, run to a marker, and then run back. Then you run to a marker that\u2019s a little farther away and run back. This keeps going until you\u2019ve reached the other side of the gym or the field and you\u2019re out of breath.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">You\u2019re going to have your CoDrone Mini do the same thing! Set a variable to 1 after takeoff. Program your drone to fly forward for the value in the variable and then fly back for the amount in the variable. Next, increase the value in the variable and repeat. Your drone should do 4 shuttle runs, so make sure you have enough room to fly!<\/span>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 8 Getting Started [vc_column_text]Make sure that your CoDrone Mini is paired before starting the lesson! If you forgot how to do this, make sure to review how to&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/variables\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":1687,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":407,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[1814,1817,1763,1777,1737,1779,1793,626,1533,1438,853,144,167,171,63],"lesson-tag":[2108,2125,2123,2124,2122,2117],"class_list":["post-523","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-blockly","lesson-tag-codrone-mini","lesson-tag-fundamentals","lesson-tag-junior","lesson-tag-programming","lesson-tag-variables","module-junior","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/523","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/lesson"}],"version-history":[{"count":10,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/523\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":12814,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/523\/revisions\/12814"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media\/1687"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=523"}],"wp:term":[{"taxonomy":"lesson-category","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-category?post=523"},{"taxonomy":"lesson-tag","embeddable":true